.SH NAME
linscp\-cli \- console and scripting client for SFTP, SCP, FTP, WebDAV and S3
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY linscp\-cli
[\fIsession\fR] [\fB/script=\fR\fIfile\fR] [\fB/command\fR \fIcommand\fR...] [\fB/parameter\fR \fB//\fR \fIvalue\fR...] [\fIswitch\fR...]
.YS
.SY linscp\-cli
\fB/keygen\fR \fIkey\-file\fR [\fB/output=\fR\fIfile\fR] [\fB/changepassphrase\fR] [\fB/comment=\fR\fItext\fR] [\fB/certificate=\fR\fIfile\fR]
.YS
.SY linscp\-cli
\fB/copyid\fR \fB/identity=\fR\fIkey\-file\fR \fIsite\fR
.YS
.SY linscp\-cli
\fB/fingerprintscan\fR \fIsite\fR
.YS
.SY linscp\-cli
\fB/batchsettings\fR \fIsite\-mask\fR \fIname\fR\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR...
.YS
.SY linscp\-cli
\fB/info\fR
.YS
.SY linscp\-cli
\fB/help\fR
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBlinscp\-cli\fR transfers files between the local computer and remote servers over SFTP, SCP, FTP (plain or with TLS), WebDAV and Amazon S3.
It runs commands typed at its \fBlinscp>\fR prompt, read from a script file (\fB/script\fR), given on the command line (\fB/command\fR) or piped to its standard input.
It shares sites, settings and host keys with \fBlinscp\fR(1).
.PP
The optional \fIsession\fR names a site saved in the Site Manager, a folder or workspace of sites, or a session URL of the form \fIprotocol\fR\fB://\fR[\fIuser\fR[\fB:\fR\fIpassword\fR]\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fR[\fB:\fR\fIport\fR][\fB/\fR\fIpath\fR], where \fIprotocol\fR is one of \fBsftp\fR, \fBscp\fR, \fBftp\fR, \fBftps\fR, \fBftpes\fR, \fBdav\fR, \fBdavs\fR or \fBs3\fR.
When given, \fBlinscp\-cli\fR connects to it before it runs any command.
.PP
Switches start with a slash or a hyphen:
\fB/script=job.txt\fR and \fB\-script=job.txt\fR are the same.
Their names are not case-sensitive.
A value follows an equals sign; switches that take a list (\fB/command\fR, \fB/parameter\fR, \fB/rawsettings\fR, \fB/rawconfig\fR) take the arguments that follow, up to the next switch.
Unknown switches are ignored.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB/privatekey=\fR\fIfile\fR
SSH private key to authenticate with when connecting to the session given on the command line.
.TP
\fB/hostkey=\fR\fIfingerprint\fR
Fingerprint the server's SSH host key must have; the connection is refused if it differs.
.TP
\fB/username=\fR\fIuser\fR
User name to log in with, used instead of one in the site or URL.
.TP
\fB/password=\fR\fIpassword\fR
Password to log in with.
Other users of the machine may see it in the process list, so prefer \fB/passwordsfromfiles\fR.
.TP
\fB/passwordsfromfiles\fR
Treat the values of the password and passphrase switches as names of files that hold the secret.
.TP
\fB/clientcert=\fR\fIfile\fR
TLS client certificate file, for FTPS and WebDAV servers that ask for one.
.TP
\fB/certificate=\fR\fIfingerprint\fR
Fingerprint the server's TLS certificate must have, for FTPS and WebDAVS.
.TP
\fB/passphrase=\fR\fIpassphrase\fR
Passphrase that unlocks an encrypted private key or client certificate.
.TP
\fB/passive\fR[\fB=\fR\fIon/off\fR]
Turn FTP passive mode on (the default) or, with \fB=off\fR, use active mode.
.TP
\fB/implicit\fR
Use implicit TLS for FTP, usually on port 990.
.TP
\fB/explicit\fR
Use explicit TLS for FTP, upgrading the plain connection with \fBAUTH\fR \fBTLS\fR.
.TP
\fB/timeout=\fR\fIseconds\fR
How many seconds to wait for a server reply before giving up.
.TP
\fB/rawsettings\fR \fIname\fR\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR...
Set any site setting by its raw name, as stored in the configuration file.
.TP
\fB/script=\fR\fIfile\fR
Run the commands in a text file.
If the file does not end with \fBexit\fR, the prompt stays open afterwards.
.TP
\fB/command\fR \fIcommand\fR...
Run each following argument as one command.
Quote every command that contains spaces.
.TP
\fB/parameter\fR \fB//\fR \fIvalue\fR...
Pass values to the script, where \fB%1%\fR, \fB%2%\fR and so on stand for them.
The \fB//\fR ends the list of switches.
.TP
\fB/stdout\fR[\fB=binary/chunked\fR]
Let \fBget\fR write a file to standard output when you give \fB\-\fR as the target.
Progress and messages then go to standard error.
.TP
\fB/stdin\fR[\fB=binary\fR]
Let \fBput\fR read file content from standard input when you give \fB\-\fR as the source.
.TP
\fB/log=\fR\fIfile\fR
Write a session log to this file.
.TP
\fB/loglevel=\fR\fIlevel\fR
How much detail the log holds, from \fB0\fR to \fB2\fR.
A trailing \fB*\fR also records passwords, so keep such logs private.
.TP
\fB/logsize=\fR[\fIcount\fR\fB*\fR]\fIsize\fR
Start a new log file when the current one reaches the size (such as \fB10M\fR), keeping at most the given number of old files.
.TP
\fB/xmllog=\fR\fIfile\fR
Write an XML log of each operation and its result to this file, for other programs to read.
.TP
\fB/xmlgroups\fR
Wrap the XML log entries of each command in one group element.
.TP
\fB/xmllogrequired\fR
With \fB/xmllog\fR, fail when the XML log cannot be written, instead of going on without it.
.TP
\fB/nointeractiveinput\fR
Cancel any prompt instead of waiting for keyboard input.
Use it in cron jobs and pipelines.
.TP
\fB/ini=\fR\fIfile\fR
Read and save settings in this file instead of the default configuration file (see FILES).
With \fBnul\fR, start from defaults and save nothing.
.TP
\fB/rawconfig\fR \fIname\fR\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR...
Set any application setting by its raw name. linscp-cli saves its configuration when it exits, so the value stays.
.TP
\fB/batchsettings\fR \fIsite\-mask\fR \fIname\fR\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR...
Change raw settings on every saved site whose name matches the mask, then save the sites.
Prints which sites changed.
.TP
\fB/copyid\fR \fB/identity=\fR\fIkey\-file\fR \fIsite\fR
Add the public half of a key to \fB~/.ssh/authorized_keys\fR on the server, like \fBssh\-copy\-id\fR.
Needs \fB/identity\fR and a site.
.TP
\fB/identity=\fR\fIkey\-file\fR
With \fB/copyid\fR, the key file whose public key gets installed.
\fB\-i\fR is accepted as a short form.
.TP
\fB/fingerprintscan\fR \fIsite\fR
Connect to an SSH or FTPS server only long enough to print its host key or certificate fingerprints (SHA-256, SHA-1, MD5), without checking them.
Compare the result with a value you trust before you rely on it.
.TP
\fB/keygen\fR \fIkey\-file\fR
Convert \fIkey\-file\fR, an OpenSSH or other private key, to the PPK format that linscp uses, or change an existing PPK file.
The switches below modify it.
Then exit.
.TP
\fB/output=\fR\fIfile\fR
With \fB/keygen\fR, where to write the converted key.
.TP
\fB/changepassphrase\fR
With \fB/keygen\fR, ask for a new passphrase for the key.
.TP
\fB/comment=\fR\fItext\fR
With \fB/keygen\fR, set the comment stored in the key.
.TP
\fB/certificate=\fR\fIfile\fR
With \fB/keygen\fR, add this OpenSSH certificate to the key.
.TP
\fB/info\fR
Print the SSH and TLS libraries linscp uses and the ciphers, key exchanges, host key types and MACs they offer, then exit.
.TP
\fB/help\fR
Show a short usage summary and exit.
.SH SCRIPTS
.PP
A script has one command per line.
Lines starting with \fB#\fR are comments.
Put arguments that contain spaces in double quotes; write a double quote inside quotes as two.
.PP
\fB%NAME%\fR is replaced by the value of the environment variable \fBNAME\fR, and \fB%1%\fR, \fB%2%\fR and so on by the values given after \fB/parameter\fR \fB//\fR.
\fB%TIMESTAMP#yyyy\-mm\-dd%\fR becomes the current local time in the given format (\fByyyy\fR, \fBmm\fR, \fBdd\fR, \fBhh\fR, \fBnn\fR for minutes, \fBss\fR); an offset such as \fB%TIMESTAMP\-1D#yyyymmdd%\fR shifts it.
.PP
With \fB/script\fR or \fB/command\fR, \fBlinscp\-cli\fR starts with \fBoption\fR \fBbatch\fR \fBabort\fR and \fBoption\fR \fBconfirm\fR \fBoff\fR, so a script stops at the first error and overwrites files without asking.
Commands typed or piped in start with \fBoption\fR \fBbatch\fR \fBoff\fR and \fBoption\fR \fBconfirm\fR \fBon\fR.
.SH COMMANDS
.PP
These are the commands that \fBlinscp\-cli\fR accepts at its prompt, in a script file passed with \fB/script\fR, or as arguments to \fB/command\fR.
.SS call
.nf
\fBcall\fR \fIcommand\fR
.fi
.PP
With SFTP and SCP, \fBcall\fR runs \fIcommand\fR in a shell on the server and prints what it writes.
If the current connection cannot run commands, linscp opens a second shell session for it with the same login.
.PP
With FTP, \fBcall\fR sends \fIcommand\fR to the server as it is, for example a \fBSITE\fR command.
.PP
The command runs without a terminal, so it must not wait for input.
Use \fBchecksum\fR or \fBchmod\fR where they fit; they work without a shell.
.PP
Also available as \fB!\fR.
.PP
Example: unpack an archive you just uploaded.
.RS 4
.EX
call tar \-xzf /var/www/release.tar.gz \-C /var/www/html
.EE
.RE
.SS cd
.nf
\fBcd\fR [\fIremote\-directory\fR]
.fi
.PP
Moves the active session to \fIremote\-directory\fR and prints the new path.
Relative paths start from the current remote directory.
Without an argument, \fBcd\fR goes to your home directory on the server.
.PP
Commands that take remote paths, such as \fBget\fR and \fBls\fR, resolve relative names against this directory.
To change the local directory, use \fBlcd\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.EX
cd /var/www/html
cd ..
cd
.EE
.RE
.SS checksum
.nf
\fBchecksum\fR \fIalgorithm\fR \fIremote\-file\fR
.fi
.PP
Computes a hash of \fIremote\-file\fR on the server and prints it followed by the file name.
The file is not downloaded.
.PP
Which algorithms work depends on the server.
With SFTP the server must offer hashing, or linscp falls back to a shell session and the usual command-line tools.
Common names are \fBmd5\fR, \fBsha\-1\fR and \fBsha\-256\fR.
.PP
Only a single file is accepted; a directory or a mask that matches several files is an error.
.PP
Example:
.RS 4
.EX
checksum sha\-256 /srv/backups/db.dump
.EE
.RE
.SS chmod
.nf
\fBchmod\fR \fImode\fR \fIremote\-file\fR...
.fi
.PP
Sets the permissions of each named file to \fImode\fR, an octal number of three digits (\fB644\fR) or four, where the first digit holds the setuid, setgid and sticky bits (\fB1777\fR).
.PP
The last part of each name may be a mask, such as \fB*.sh\fR.
A mask that matches nothing is handled as set by \fBfailonnomatch\fR.
.PP
The server must support permissions; SFTP and SCP do, and many FTP servers accept \fBSITE\fR \fBCHMOD\fR.
.PP
Affected by the script option \fBfailonnomatch\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.EX
chmod 755 /home/alice/bin/deploy.sh
chmod 640 /var/www/html/*.php
.EE
.RE
.SS close
.nf
\fBclose\fR [\fInumber\fR]
.fi
.PP
Disconnects the session with the given number, as listed by \fBsession\fR.
Without a number, \fBclose\fR disconnects the active session.
.PP
To end the program and close every session at once, use \fBexit\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.EX
close
close 2
.EE
.RE
.SS cp
.nf
\fBcp\fR \fIremote\-file\fR... \fIremote\-target\fR
.fi
.PP
Duplicates files on the server without downloading them.
The last argument says where the copies go:
.IP \(bu 2
a directory, written with a trailing \fB/\fR (\fB/srv/backup/\fR), keeps the names;
.IP \(bu 2
a plain name (\fBindex.old\fR) gives a single copy that name;
.IP \(bu 2
an operation mask (\fB*.bak\fR) renames each copy.
.PP
You can combine a directory with a name or mask:
\fB/srv/backup/*.bak\fR.
The sources may contain masks too.
\fBcp\fR does not replace a file that already exists at the target.
.PP
The server must support copying:
SFTP servers with the copy extension, or any server where linscp can open a shell.
.PP
Affected by the script option \fBfailonnomatch\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.EX
cp /var/www/html/index.html /var/www/html/index.html.orig
cp /etc/nginx/*.conf /home/alice/nginx\-backup/
.EE
.RE
.SS echo
.nf
\fBecho\fR \fItext\fR
.fi
.PP
Writes \fItext\fR to the output exactly as you typed it, quotes included.
Use it to label sections of a long script's output or to print script parameters such as \fB%1%\fR.
.PP
Example:
.RS 4
.EX
echo Uploading release %1%...
.EE
.RE
.SS exit
.nf
\fBexit\fR
.fi
.PP
Disconnects every open session and ends the program.
In a script, put \fBexit\fR on the last line; without it, \fBlinscp\-cli\fR stays at the prompt once the file is done.
.PP
The exit code is \fB0\fR when every command succeeded and \fB1\fR when any of them failed.
Read option batch to control whether a script goes on after an error.
.PP
Also available as \fBbye\fR.
.PP
Example:
.RS 4
.EX
exit
.EE
.RE
.SS get
.nf
\fBget\fR [\fIswitches\fR] \fIremote\-file\fR... [\fIlocal\-target\fR]
.fi
.PP
Copies remote files and directories to the local computer.
Directories are copied with everything they contain.
.PP
With a single argument, the file lands in the local working directory (see \fBlcd\fR).
With more, the last argument is the target:
.IP \(bu 2
end it with \fB/\fR to name a directory:
\fB/home/alice/backup/\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
otherwise its last part is the new file name, or an operation mask such as \fB*.bak\fR.
.PP
Use \fB./\fR as the target to download several files into the local working directory.
The source names may use masks in their last part.
With \fB/stdout\fR, a target of \fB\-\fR writes the file to standard output.
.PP
Also available as \fBrecv\fR, \fBmget\fR.
.PP
Switches:
.TP
\fB\-delete\fR
Remove each remote file once it has been downloaded, which turns the copy into a move.
.TP
\fB\-resume\fR
Continue an interrupted download where it stopped (SFTP and FTP).
.TP
\fB\-append\fR
Add the downloaded data to the end of an existing local file (SFTP).
.TP
\fB\-preservetime\fR[\fB=all\fR]
Give local files the modification time of the remote ones.
With \fB=all\fR, directories keep theirs too.
.TP
\fB\-nopreservetime\fR
Let downloaded files get the current time.
.TP
\fB\-speed=\fR\fIKB/s\fR
Cap the download rate in kilobytes per second.
.TP
\fB\-transfer=\fR\fImode\fR
Use \fBbinary\fR, \fBascii\fR (convert line endings) or \fBautomatic\fR mode.
.TP
\fB\-filemask=\fR\fImask\fR
Select which files and subdirectories to download with an include and exclude mask.
.TP
\fB\-resumesupport=\fR\fIon/off/KB\fR
Download to a temporary file first, always (\fBon\fR), never (\fBoff\fR), or for files larger than the number of KB given.
.TP
\fB\-neweronly\fR
Skip files whose local copy is the same age or newer.
.TP
\fB\-latest\fR
Download only the most recently modified of the matching files.
.PP
Affected by the script options \fBconfirm\fR, \fBfailonnomatch\fR, \fBreconnecttime\fR.
.PP
Example: download one file into a local directory.
.RS 4
.EX
get /var/www/html/index.html /home/alice/backup/
.EE
.RE
.PP
Example: move the day's logs, renaming them on the way.
.RS 4
.EX
get \-delete /var/log/app/*.log /home/alice/logs/*.old
.EE
.RE
.PP
Example: fetch only the newest dump.
.RS 4
.EX
get \-latest /srv/backups/*.sql.gz ./
.EE
.RE
.SS help
.nf
\fBhelp\fR [\fIcommand\fR...]
.fi
.PP
Without arguments, \fBhelp\fR lists every command with a one-line purpose.
With command names, it prints the built-in help for each.
.PP
Some examples in the built-in help use paths of another operating system.
On Linux, local paths use \fB/\fR, and a local target directory ends with \fB/\fR.
This manual page describes the Linux behaviour.
.PP
Also available as \fBman\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.EX
help
help get put
.EE
.RE
.SS keepuptodate
.nf
\fBkeepuptodate\fR [\fIswitches\fR] [\fIlocal\-directory\fR [\fIremote\-directory\fR]]
.fi
.PP
Watches \fIlocal\-directory\fR and its subdirectories.
Each time something changes, linscp waits until the directory has been quiet for half a second, then uploads what is new or modified to the matching place under \fIremote\-directory\fR.
Without arguments it uses the local and remote working directories.
.PP
\fBkeepuptodate\fR does not upload anything that existed before it started; run \fBsynchronize\fR \fBremote\fR first if the two sides differ.
Existing remote files are replaced without asking.
.PP
The command runs until you press Ctrl+C or an error occurs, so it is usually the last command in a script.
.PP
Switches:
.TP
\fB\-delete\fR
Remove a remote file or directory after it is deleted locally.
.TP
\fB\-permissions=\fR\fImode\fR
Give uploaded files these octal permissions, such as \fB644\fR.
.TP
\fB\-nopermissions\fR
Let the server choose the permissions of uploaded files.
.TP
\fB\-speed=\fR\fIKB/s\fR
Cap the upload rate in kilobytes per second.
.TP
\fB\-transfer=\fR\fImode\fR
Use \fBbinary\fR, \fBascii\fR (convert line endings) or \fBautomatic\fR mode.
.TP
\fB\-filemask=\fR\fImask\fR
Watch and upload only what this mask selects.
.TP
\fB\-resumesupport=\fR\fIon/off/KB\fR
Upload to a temporary file first, always (\fBon\fR), never (\fBoff\fR), or for files larger than the number of KB given.
.PP
Affected by the script option \fBreconnecttime\fR.
.PP
Example:
.RS 4
.EX
keepuptodate \-delete /home/alice/site /var/www/html
.EE
.RE
.SS lcd
.nf
\fBlcd\fR \fIlocal\-directory\fR
.fi
.PP
Sets the local directory that every session uses and prints the new path.
Relative paths start from the current local directory; \fB..\fR goes up one level.
A directory is required, and \fB~\fR is not expanded, so write your home directory in full.
.PP
\fBget\fR downloads into this directory by default, and \fBput\fR resolves relative source names against it.
To see where you are, use \fBlpwd\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.EX
lcd /home/alice/site
lcd ..
.EE
.RE
.SS lls
.nf
\fBlls\fR [\fIlocal\-directory\fR][\fB/\fR\fIwildcard\fR]
.fi
.PP
Prints the contents of a local directory, one entry per line with its date, time and either its size or \fB<DIR>\fR.
Without an argument it lists the local working directory.
.PP
A wildcard in the last part, such as \fB*.html\fR, limits the list to the matching names.
On Linux this match is case-sensitive, and the size and time constraints of other masks do not apply here.
A wildcard that matches nothing is handled as set by \fBfailonnomatch\fR.
.PP
Affected by the script option \fBfailonnomatch\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.EX
lls
lls /home/alice/site/*.html
.EE
.RE
.SS ln
.nf
\fBln\fR \fItarget\fR \fIlink\fR
.fi
.PP
Creates the symbolic link \fIlink\fR, pointing to \fItarget\fR.
The order is the same as in \fBln\fR \fB\-s\fR: what the link points to comes first.
.PP
\fItarget\fR is stored as written, so a relative target is resolved from the directory that holds the link.
The server must support symbolic links, which SFTP and SCP servers normally do.
.PP
Also available as \fBsymlink\fR.
.PP
Example:
.RS 4
.EX
ln /var/www/releases/2.4.0 /var/www/current
.EE
.RE
.SS lpwd
.nf
\fBlpwd\fR
.fi
.PP
Prints the local directory that \fBget\fR, \fBput\fR and \fBlls\fR use for relative paths.
Change it with \fBlcd\fR.
.PP
Example:
.RS 4
.EX
lpwd
.EE
.RE
.SS ls
.nf
\fBls\fR [\fIremote\-directory\fR][\fB/\fR\fImask\fR]
.fi
.PP
Prints the contents of a remote directory in a long format similar to \fBls\fR \fB\-l\fR.
Without an argument it lists the remote working directory.
.PP
A mask in the last part, such as \fB*.log\fR, limits the list to matching entries.
When the mask matches nothing, the result follows \fBfailonnomatch\fR, which makes \fBls\fR a simple way to test whether a file exists.
.PP
Also available as \fBdir\fR.
.PP
Affected by the script option \fBfailonnomatch\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.EX
ls
ls /var/log/app/*.log
.EE
.RE
.SS mkdir
.nf
\fBmkdir\fR \fIremote\-directory\fR
.fi
.PP
Creates one directory.
Its parent must already exist, so create nested directories one level at a time.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.EX
mkdir /var/www/html/assets
mkdir /var/www/html/assets/img
.EE
.RE
.SS mv
.nf
\fBmv\fR \fIremote\-file\fR... \fIremote\-target\fR
.fi
.PP
Moves files and directories within the server, or renames them.
The last argument is the destination:
.IP \(bu 2
a directory with a trailing \fB/\fR keeps the names;
.IP \(bu 2
a plain name renames a single file;
.IP \(bu 2
an operation mask such as \fB*.old\fR renames each file it is applied to.
.PP
A directory and a name or mask can be combined:
\fB/srv/archive/*.old\fR.
The sources may contain masks.
\fBmv\fR does not replace an existing file at the destination.
.PP
Also available as \fBrename\fR.
.PP
Affected by the script option \fBfailonnomatch\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.EX
mv /var/www/html/index.html /var/www/html/index.old
mv /srv/incoming/*.csv /srv/processed/
.EE
.RE
.SS open
.nf
\fBopen\fR [\fIswitches\fR] \fIsite\fR
\fBopen\fR [\fIswitches\fR] \fIprotocol\fR\fB://\fR[\fIuser\fR[\fB:\fR\fIpassword\fR]\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fR[\fB:\fR\fIport\fR][\fB/\fR\fIpath\fR]
.fi
.PP
Starts a new session and makes it the active one.
Give either the name of a site saved in the Site Manager or a session URL.
The protocol is one of \fBsftp\fR, \fBscp\fR, \fBftp\fR, \fBftps\fR, \fBftpes\fR, \fBdav\fR, \fBdavs\fR or \fBs3\fR.
A path at the end of the URL becomes the starting remote directory.
.PP
Switches set or override parts of the login.
You can keep several sessions open and move between them with \fBsession\fR.
.PP
Pin the server's identity in scripts with \fB\-hostkey\fR or \fB\-certificate\fR.
\fB\-hostkey=*\fR accepts any key; it removes the protection against impostor servers, so use it only for tests.
.PP
Switches:
.TP
\fB\-privatekey=\fR\fIfile\fR
SSH private key to authenticate with.
.TP
\fB\-hostkey=\fR\fIfingerprint\fR
Fingerprint the SSH host key must match (SFTP and SCP).
.TP
\fB\-clientcert=\fR\fIfile\fR
TLS client certificate file to present (FTPS and WebDAVS).
.TP
\fB\-certificate=\fR\fIfingerprint\fR
Fingerprint the server's TLS certificate must match (FTPS and WebDAVS).
.TP
\fB\-passphrase=\fR\fIpassphrase\fR
Passphrase for an encrypted private key or client certificate.
.TP
\fB\-passive=\fR\fIon/off\fR
Use FTP passive mode (\fBon\fR) or active mode (\fBoff\fR).
.TP
\fB\-implicit\fR
Use implicit TLS for FTP.
.TP
\fB\-explicit\fR
Use explicit TLS for FTP.
.TP
\fB\-timeout=\fR\fIseconds\fR
Seconds to wait for a server reply before giving up.
.TP
\fB\-username=\fR\fIuser\fR
User name, used instead of one in the site or URL.
.TP
\fB\-password=\fR\fIpassword\fR
Password, used instead of one in the site or URL.
.TP
\fB\-rawsettings\fR \fIname\fR\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR...
Set any site setting by its raw configuration name.
.TP
\fB\-filezilla\fR
Look \fIsite\fR up in the site manager file of another file transfer client, \fB~/.config/filezilla/sitemanager.xml\fR, instead of among linscp's own sites.
.TP
\fB\-newpassword=\fR\fIpassword\fR
Change the account password to this value while logging in, when the server supports it.
.TP
\fB\-passwordsfromfiles\fR
Treat the values of \fB\-password\fR, \fB\-passphrase\fR and \fB\-newpassword\fR as paths of files that hold them.
.PP
Example: open a saved site.
.RS 4
.EX
open "Production web"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Example: connect with a key and a pinned host key.
.RS 4
.EX
open sftp://deploy@example.com/ \-privatekey=/home/alice/.ssh/deploy.ppk \-hostkey="ssh\-ed25519 255 SHA256:..."
.EE
.RE
.SS option
.nf
\fBoption\fR [\fIname\fR [\fIvalue\fR]]
.fi
.PP
With no argument, \fBoption\fR prints every script option and its current value.
With a name, it prints that one.
With a name and a value, it sets the option and prints the result.
.PP
Some options start from your linscp preferences.
Changing them here lasts only until \fBlinscp\-cli\fR exits and never touches the saved configuration.
The options are described below.
.PP
Examples: the usual start of an unattended script.
.RS 4
.EX
option batch abort
option confirm off
.EE
.RE
.SS put
.nf
\fBput\fR [\fIswitches\fR] \fIlocal\-file\fR... [\fIremote\-target\fR]
.fi
.PP
Copies local files and directories to the server.
Directories are copied with everything they contain.
.PP
With a single argument, the file goes to the remote working directory (see \fBcd\fR).
With more, the last argument is the target:
.IP \(bu 2
end it with \fB/\fR to name a directory:
\fB/var/www/html/\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
otherwise its last part is the new name, or an operation mask such as \fB*.bak\fR.
.PP
Use \fB./\fR as the target to upload several files into the remote working directory.
Wildcards in local source names follow the local file system, so they are case-sensitive on Linux.
With \fB/stdin\fR, a source of \fB\-\fR uploads standard input to the single file named by the target.
.PP
Also available as \fBsend\fR, \fBmput\fR.
.PP
Switches:
.TP
\fB\-delete\fR
Remove each local file once it has been uploaded, which turns the copy into a move.
.TP
\fB\-resume\fR
Continue an interrupted upload where it stopped (SFTP and FTP).
.TP
\fB\-append\fR
Add the uploaded data to the end of an existing remote file (SFTP).
.TP
\fB\-preservetime\fR[\fB=all\fR]
Give remote files the modification time of the local ones.
With \fB=all\fR, directories keep theirs too.
.TP
\fB\-nopreservetime\fR
Let uploaded files get the current time.
.TP
\fB\-permissions=\fR\fImode\fR
Give uploaded files these octal permissions, such as \fB644\fR.
.TP
\fB\-nopermissions\fR
Let the server choose the permissions of uploaded files.
.TP
\fB\-speed=\fR\fIKB/s\fR
Cap the upload rate in kilobytes per second.
.TP
\fB\-transfer=\fR\fImode\fR
Use \fBbinary\fR, \fBascii\fR (convert line endings) or \fBautomatic\fR mode.
.TP
\fB\-filemask=\fR\fImask\fR
Select which files and subdirectories to upload with an include and exclude mask.
.TP
\fB\-resumesupport=\fR\fIon/off/KB\fR
Upload to a temporary file first, always (\fBon\fR), never (\fBoff\fR), or for files larger than the number of KB given.
.TP
\fB\-neweronly\fR
Skip files whose remote copy is the same age or newer.
.TP
\fB\-latest\fR
Upload only the most recently modified of the matching files.
.PP
Affected by the script options \fBconfirm\fR, \fBfailonnomatch\fR, \fBreconnecttime\fR.
.PP
Example: publish a page with fixed permissions.
.RS 4
.EX
put \-permissions=644 /home/alice/site/index.html /var/www/html/
.EE
.RE
.PP
Example: upload a directory tree, leaving out temporary files and Git data.
.RS 4
.EX
put \-filemask="|*.tmp; .git/" /home/alice/site/ /var/www/html/
.EE
.RE
.PP
Example: stream a database dump straight to the server (needs \fB/stdin\fR).
.RS 4
.EX
put \- /srv/backups/db.sql
.EE
.RE
.SS pwd
.nf
\fBpwd\fR
.fi
.PP
Prints the current directory of the active session.
Relative remote paths in other commands start from here; change it with \fBcd\fR.
.PP
Example:
.RS 4
.EX
pwd
.EE
.RE
.SS rm
.nf
\fBrm\fR \fIremote\-file\fR...
.fi
.PP
Deletes each named file.
A named directory is deleted together with everything inside it.
The last part of each name may be a mask.
.PP
When the site has a remote recycle bin configured, \fBrm\fR moves the files there instead of deleting them.
.PP
A mask that matches nothing is handled as set by \fBfailonnomatch\fR.
.PP
Affected by the script option \fBfailonnomatch\fR.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.EX
rm /var/www/html/old.html
rm /tmp/upload\-*.part
.EE
.RE
.SS rmdir
.nf
\fBrmdir\fR \fIremote\-directory\fR...
.fi
.PP
Deletes each named directory.
Most servers refuse unless the directory is empty; to delete a directory with its contents, use \fBrm\fR.
\fBrmdir\fR accepts only directories, which guards against deleting a file by mistake.
.PP
When the site has a remote recycle bin configured, the directories are moved there instead.
.PP
Example:
.RS 4
.EX
rmdir /var/www/releases/2.3.0
.EE
.RE
.SS session
.nf
\fBsession\fR [\fInumber\fR]
.fi
.PP
Without a number, \fBsession\fR lists the open sessions with their numbers.
With a number, it makes that session active, so the next remote commands go to it.
.PP
Each \fBopen\fR adds a session and makes it active.
.PP
Examples:
.RS 4
.EX
session
session 1
.EE
.RE
.SS stat
.nf
\fBstat\fR \fIremote\-file\fR
.fi
.PP
Prints a single listing line for \fIremote\-file\fR: permissions, owner, group, size, modification time and name, in the same format as \fBls\fR.
It fails when the file does not exist, which a script can use as a test.
.PP
Example:
.RS 4
.EX
stat /var/www/html/index.html
.EE
.RE
.SS synchronize
.nf
\fBsynchronize\fR \fBlocal\fR|\fBremote\fR|\fBboth\fR [\fIswitches\fR] [\fIlocal\-directory\fR [\fIremote\-directory\fR]]
.fi
.PP
Compares the two directory trees and copies what differs.
The first argument picks the direction:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBremote\fR uploads, so the server matches your computer;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlocal\fR downloads, so your computer matches the server;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBboth\fR copies newer files in each direction.
.PP
Without directories, the local and remote working directories are used.
Files are replaced without asking.
By default only files that are new or newer on the source side are copied; \fB\-mirror\fR and \fB\-criteria\fR change that.
.PP
Try a new command with \fB\-preview\fR first, especially together with \fB\-delete\fR.
.PP
Switches:
.TP
\fB\-preview\fR
List what would be copied or deleted, and change nothing.
.TP
\fB\-delete\fR
Delete files and directories in the target that the source does not have.
Ignored with \fBboth\fR.
.TP
\fB\-mirror\fR
Copy every file that differs, even when the target copy is newer.
Ignored with \fBboth\fR.
.TP
\fB\-criteria=\fR\fIcriteria\fR
How files are compared.
\fBtime\fR, \fBsize\fR and \fBchecksum\fR can be combined with commas, such as \fBtime,size\fR; \fBnone\fR copies only missing files; \fBeither\fR means \fBtime,size\fR.
Ignored with \fBboth\fR.
.TP
\fB\-permissions=\fR\fImode\fR
Give uploaded files these octal permissions, such as \fB644\fR.
.TP
\fB\-nopermissions\fR
Let the server choose the permissions of uploaded files.
.TP
\fB\-speed=\fR\fIKB/s\fR
Cap the transfer rate in kilobytes per second.
.TP
\fB\-transfer=\fR\fImode\fR
Use \fBbinary\fR, \fBascii\fR (convert line endings) or \fBautomatic\fR mode.
.TP
\fB\-filemask=\fR\fImask\fR
Limit the comparison to what this mask selects.
.TP
\fB\-resumesupport=\fR\fIon/off/KB\fR
Transfer to a temporary file first, always (\fBon\fR), never (\fBoff\fR), or for files larger than the number of KB given.
.PP
Affected by the script option \fBreconnecttime\fR.
.PP
Example: see what an upload would change.
.RS 4
.EX
synchronize remote \-preview \-delete /home/alice/site /var/www/html
.EE
.RE
.PP
Example: back up a server directory, comparing by size as well as time.
.RS 4
.EX
synchronize local \-criteria=time,size /home/alice/backup/www /var/www
.EE
.RE
.SH MASKS
.PP
A file mask selects files by name.
\fB*\fR matches any run of characters, \fB?\fR one character, and [\fBa\-z\fR] one character of a set or range.
Masks ignore letter case, except the wildcard in a local source of \fBput\fR or \fBlls\fR, which the file system matches.
.PP
A size or time constraint can follow the name part:
\fB>500K\fR, \fB<2G\fR, \fB>2026\-01\-01\fR, \fB>7D\fR (modified within seven days), \fB<12H\fR; \fB>=\fR and \fB<=\fR include the limit.
A mask ending in \fB/\fR matches directories, and a path before the name matches by location (\fB*/logs/*.txt\fR, \fB./build/\fR).
.PP
In \fB\-filemask\fR, separate masks with a semicolon or a comma, and split include from exclude masks with |:
\fB\-filemask="*.php;\fR \fB*.inc\fR | \fBtest_*"\fR and \fB\-filemask="\fR| \fB.git/;\fR \fB*.tmp"\fR.
A name given to a command such as \fBget\fR or \fBrm\fR takes one simple mask in its last part.
.PP
When the target of \fBget\fR, \fBput\fR, \fBcp\fR or \fBmv\fR is not a plain name, it is an operation mask that renames each file: with \fB*.bak\fR, \fBindex.html\fR becomes \fBindex.bak\fR; with \fBold_*\fR, it becomes \fBold_index.html\fR.
.SH SCRIPT OPTIONS
Set with \fBoption\fR \fIname\fR \fIvalue\fR.
.TP
\fBecho\fR \fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR
With \fBon\fR, \fBlinscp\-cli\fR prints each command before it runs it.
This helps when you read the output of a long script.
The default is \fBoff\fR.
.TP
\fBbatch\fR \fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR | \fBabort\fR | \fBcontinue\fR
Controls what happens when a command needs an answer or fails.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBoff\fR (default):
\fBlinscp\-cli\fR asks you.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBon\fR: every question gets a negative answer, so the script never waits.
Combine it with \fBoption\fR \fBconfirm\fR \fBoff\fR, or existing files are not overwritten.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBabort\fR: questions are answered as with \fBon\fR, and the first error stops the script with exit code 1.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcontinue\fR: errors are reported and the script goes on.
.RE
.IP
Choosing any batch mode also limits \fBreconnecttime\fR to 120 seconds if it was \fBoff\fR.
.TP
\fBconfirm\fR \fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR
With \fBon\fR (default), \fBget\fR and \fBput\fR ask before they overwrite an existing file.
Set it to \fBoff\fR in scripts that should replace files without asking.
.TP
\fBreconnecttime\fR \fBoff\fR | \fIseconds\fR
How long \fBlinscp\-cli\fR keeps trying to restore a dropped connection during a transfer or synchronization before it reports an error.
\fBoff\fR (default) means no limit.
.TP
\fBfailonnomatch\fR \fBon\fR | \fBoff\fR
Decides whether a mask that matches nothing is an error.
With \fBoff\fR (default), the command prints a message and the script continues.
With \fBon\fR, the command fails, which stops a script running in \fBbatch\fR \fBabort\fR mode.
It applies to \fBget\fR, \fBput\fR, \fBrm\fR, \fBmv\fR, \fBcp\fR, \fBchmod\fR, \fBls\fR and \fBlls\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB~/.config/linscp/WinSCP.ini\fR
Sites, preferences and remembered host keys, shared with \fBlinscp\fR(1).
The directory is \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/linscp\fR when that variable is set.
It is created when needed.
.TP
\fIprogram\-directory\fR\fB/WinSCP.ini\fR
When this file exists beside the program, it is used instead, which makes a portable copy.
For an AppImage, the file goes beside the \fB.AppImage\fR file.
.TP
\fB~/.var/app/org.linscp.linscp/config/linscp/WinSCP.ini\fR
The configuration file of the Flatpak, which shares nothing with other installs and never uses a file beside its program.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The base directory of the configuration file; \fB~/.config\fR when unset.
.TP
.B HOME
The user's home directory.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Temporary files go to a private per-user directory under it; \fB/tmp\fR when unset.
.TP
.B APPIMAGE
Set by an AppImage to its own path.
A \fBWinSCP.ini\fR beside that file makes the configuration portable.
.TP
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
The SSH agent used for public key authentication.
.TP
.B WINSCP_PATH
Set by linscp, for scripts and the programs they run, to the directory that holds the program (or its \fBdotnet\fR subdirectory, when the package has the .NET scripting assembly).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every command succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
A command failed and stopped the script, a prompt was answered with Abort, or the connection could not be opened.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Upload a file to a saved site, unattended:
.RS 4
.EX
linscp\-cli /nointeractiveinput \e
    /command 'open mysite' 'put report.pdf /srv/reports/' 'exit'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a script with a session log, and pass it two values:
.RS 4
.EX
linscp\-cli /script=fetch.txt /log=fetch.log \e
    /parameter // reports /home/alice/incoming
.EE
.RE
.PP
A script that mirrors a directory to the server:
.RS 4
.EX
open sftp://deploy@example.com \-hostkey="ssh\-ed25519 255 SHA256:..."
option batch abort
option confirm off
synchronize remote \-delete /home/alice/site /var/www/html
exit
.EE
.RE
.PP
Stream a remote file to another program:
.RS 4
.EX
linscp\-cli /stdout /command 'open mysite' 'get app.log \-' 'exit' |
    grep ERROR
.EE
.RE
.PP
Convert an OpenSSH key for use with linscp:
.RS 4
.EX
linscp\-cli /keygen ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 /output=id_ed25519.ppk
.EE
.RE
